1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,168
A child runs in with news: I'll tell you how the sun rose!

2
00:00:04,168 --> 00:00:06,875
And out it comes, in a rush of pictures.

3
00:00:07,100 --> 00:00:10,096
Then, halfway through, the voice drops.

4
00:00:10,096 --> 00:00:11,854
But how he set, I know not.

5
00:00:11,854 --> 00:00:17,766
Sixteen short lines, and one of the quietest, strangest poems in your course.

6
00:00:17,766 --> 00:00:21,276
What happened at sunset that the speaker cannot describe?

7
00:00:26,767 --> 00:00:32,033
Welcome to Class 12 English, Unit 8: A Day, by Emily Dickinson.

8
00:00:34,400 --> 00:00:35,534
Six parts.

9
00:00:35,534 --> 00:00:36,863
Emily Dickinson.

10
00:00:36,863 --> 00:00:38,581
The sunrise, told.

11
00:00:38,581 --> 00:00:40,560
The sunset, not known.

12
00:00:40,560 --> 00:00:41,745
The themes.

13
00:00:41,745 --> 00:00:43,659
Form, metre and rhyme.

14
00:00:43,659 --> 00:00:45,233
And the devices you should quote.

15
00:00:49,567 --> 00:00:55,910
Emily Dickinson lived almost her entire life in one house in Amherst, Massachusetts.

16
00:00:55,910 --> 00:01:02,368
In later years she rarely went out, wore white, and spoke to some visitors from behind a door.

17
00:01:02,368 --> 00:01:06,457
She wrote about eighteen hundred poems and published perhaps ten.

18
00:01:06,457 --> 00:01:12,043
The rest she sewed into little booklets, which her sister found after her death.

19
00:01:12,043 --> 00:01:14,673
The first selection was printed in eighteen ninety.

20
00:01:14,900 --> 00:01:28,639
Her mind was shaped by the Metaphysical poets, who yoked huge ideas to homely objects; by the Bible's Book of Revelation; by the Puritan religion of New England; and by the hymns she sang every Sunday.

21
00:01:28,639 --> 00:01:30,957
You will find all four in this poem.

22
00:01:32,167 --> 00:01:34,291
Three examinable facts.

23
00:01:34,291 --> 00:01:40,346
The title, A Day, is not hers: her editors added it in eighteen ninety.

24
00:01:40,346 --> 00:01:43,288
The poem was written around eighteen sixty.

25
00:01:43,288 --> 00:01:49,060
And the mark at the end of the first line is her famous dash, a held breath rather than a stop.

26
00:01:53,833 --> 00:01:54,915
Stanza one.

27
00:01:54,915 --> 00:02:00,918
I'll tell you how the sun rose: the voice of a child who has seen something and cannot keep it.

28
00:02:00,918 --> 00:02:08,859
A ribbon at a time: dawn is not sudden, it is unrolled in narrow bands, like ribbon off a bolt in a draper's shop.

29
00:02:09,067 --> 00:02:11,412
The steeples swam in amethyst.

30
00:02:11,412 --> 00:02:16,165
Church spires are the tallest things in town, so they catch the light first.

31
00:02:16,165 --> 00:02:26,126
Amethyst is a violet jewel, and in the Book of Revelation it is one of the stones in the wall of heaven, so for a moment the town is built into heaven.

32
00:02:26,126 --> 00:02:29,186
And swam turns the air into water.

33
00:02:29,186 --> 00:02:33,418
Then the only simile in the poem: the news like squirrels ran.

34
00:02:33,418 --> 00:02:37,682
The light runs down into the streets in bursts, like a squirrel.

35
00:02:38,900 --> 00:02:40,086
Stanza two.

36
00:02:40,086 --> 00:02:52,300
The hills untied their bonnets: before dawn the hilltops are wrapped in mist, and as the light comes the mist loosens and lifts, like women untying their bonnets when they come indoors.

37
00:02:52,300 --> 00:02:59,982
The bobolinks begun: bobolinks are songbirds, and begun instead of began is a child's grammar, kept on purpose.

38
00:02:59,982 --> 00:03:01,011
Begun what?

39
00:03:01,011 --> 00:03:03,831
Singing; the speaker is too excited to say.

40
00:03:04,033 --> 00:03:05,428
Then the voice drops.

41
00:03:05,428 --> 00:03:09,178
Then I said softly to myself: That must have been the sun!

42
00:03:09,178 --> 00:03:15,714
She has watched the whole performance, and only worked out afterwards, by deduction, what caused it.

43
00:03:15,714 --> 00:03:21,376
It is charming, and it is the first sign that this speaker understands less than she describes.

44
00:03:26,167 --> 00:03:28,434
Stanza three is the turn.

45
00:03:28,434 --> 00:03:30,192
But how he set, I know not.

46
00:03:30,192 --> 00:03:33,630
Eight lines of confident telling, and now, I know not.

47
00:03:33,630 --> 00:03:40,635
Notice the sun has become he: a person, with a will, gone somewhere she cannot follow.

48
00:03:40,635 --> 00:03:44,463
And everything is hedged: there seemed a purple stile.

49
00:03:44,463 --> 00:03:50,413
A stile is a set of steps built over a field wall, so people can cross where animals cannot.

50
00:03:50,413 --> 00:03:54,522
The horizon at evening becomes a boundary, with a way over it.

51
00:03:54,733 --> 00:04:05,308
Which little yellow boys and girls were climbing all the while: the last golden rays, or small gold lit clouds, seen as children going home over the wall, one at a time.

52
00:04:06,533 --> 00:04:07,706
Stanza four.

53
00:04:07,706 --> 00:04:16,378
Till when they reached the other side, a dominie in gray: a dominie is a schoolmaster, and in America also a minister.

54
00:04:16,378 --> 00:04:21,131
In grey: the colour of dusk, and of a Puritan minister's coat.

55
00:04:21,131 --> 00:04:22,628
He has no face.

56
00:04:22,628 --> 00:04:30,115
He put gently up the evening bars: the bars are the poles that close a gap in a farm fence once the animals are in for the night.

57
00:04:30,115 --> 00:04:33,605
It is done gently; the poem insists on that.

58
00:04:33,605 --> 00:04:35,102
And led the flock away.

59
00:04:35,102 --> 00:04:41,989
The children have become sheep, the schoolmaster a shepherd: the oldest Christian image for God gathering the dead.

60
00:04:46,767 --> 00:04:48,136
The central idea.

61
00:04:48,136 --> 00:04:50,362
Behind the day lies a life.

62
00:04:50,362 --> 00:04:54,412
Morning, birth and childhood, can be told perfectly.

63
00:04:54,412 --> 00:04:57,276
Evening, death, can only be imagined.

64
00:04:57,276 --> 00:04:59,594
The word death never appears.

65
00:04:59,594 --> 00:05:07,864
Instead we get a boundary, a crossing, the other side, a figure in grey, a gate shut gently, and a flock led home.

66
00:05:09,067 --> 00:05:10,227
Four themes.

67
00:05:10,227 --> 00:05:19,993
Innocence to experience: the confident child of the first half becomes someone who has seen what she cannot explain, and it happens exactly at I know not.

68
00:05:19,993 --> 00:05:25,826
Nature as theatre: she never looks at the sun itself, only at what it does.

69
00:05:25,826 --> 00:05:33,522
Death, handled gently: Dickinson wrote dozens of poems about dying, and this is one of the few that is not afraid.

70
00:05:33,522 --> 00:05:38,938
And faith and doubt: the church and the shepherd are all there, but only as what seemed.

71
00:05:38,938 --> 00:05:41,326
A comforting picture, and an honest verb.

72
00:05:46,100 --> 00:05:46,961
The form.

73
00:05:46,961 --> 00:05:49,747
Four quatrains, sixteen lines.

74
00:05:49,747 --> 00:05:56,805
In each stanza only the second and fourth lines rhyme: a b c b. Begun, sun.

75
00:05:56,805 --> 00:05:57,898
Stile, while.

76
00:05:57,898 --> 00:05:58,914
Gray, away.

77
00:05:58,914 --> 00:06:07,449
Only the first stanza's time and ran is a slant rhyme, a near miss, which suits that breathless, excited opening.

78
00:06:08,667 --> 00:06:18,460
The metre is the common metre of the hymn book, the metre of Amazing Grace: lines of eight and six syllables, iambic tetrameter and trimeter.

79
00:06:18,460 --> 00:06:21,143
Dickinson sang it every Sunday.

80
00:06:21,143 --> 00:06:26,755
But count: in the first three stanzas the opening line is one syllable short.

81
00:06:26,755 --> 00:06:30,908
It is headless, starting on a stress, a little push forward.

82
00:06:30,908 --> 00:06:37,757
Only the last stanza fills out to perfect hymn metre, exactly as the poem becomes religious.

83
00:06:37,757 --> 00:06:38,855
That is not an accident.

84
00:06:43,633 --> 00:06:45,145
Devices to quote.

85
00:06:45,145 --> 00:06:47,163
Metaphor: a ribbon at a time.

86
00:06:47,163 --> 00:06:51,200
The poem's only simile: the news like squirrels ran.

87
00:06:51,200 --> 00:06:55,379
Personification: the hills untied their bonnets.

88
00:06:55,379 --> 00:06:59,390
Antithesis between how the sun rose and how he set.

89
00:06:59,390 --> 00:07:03,114
The allegory of the yellow children who become a flock.

90
00:07:03,114 --> 00:07:06,799
A biblical allusion to the Good Shepherd, never named.

91
00:07:06,799 --> 00:07:12,790
And enjambment: the sentence steps over the stanza break, just as the children step over the stile.

92
00:07:15,200 --> 00:07:15,970
Pause and think.

93
00:07:23,267 --> 00:07:30,333
Dominie means schoolmaster or minister; as the shepherd who leads the flock away, he suggests God or Death.

94
00:07:32,333 --> 00:07:32,768
One more.

95
00:07:40,400 --> 00:07:46,345
abcb: only lines 2 and 4 rhyme, and stanza 1's time / ran is a slant rhyme.

96
00:07:48,533 --> 00:07:49,628
Quick recap.

97
00:07:49,628 --> 00:07:53,183
Dickinson, and a poem from about eighteen sixty.

98
00:07:53,183 --> 00:07:56,009
The sunrise told in four pictures.

99
00:07:56,009 --> 00:08:01,725
The sunset only imagined: a stile, children, a grey shepherd.

100
00:08:01,725 --> 00:08:04,928
A day as a life, and death handled gently.

101
00:08:04,928 --> 00:08:06,256
And the hymn metre underneath.

102
00:08:10,433 --> 00:08:11,411
That was A Day.

103
00:08:11,411 --> 00:08:15,291
Next poem: Every Morning I Wake, by Dylan Thomas.

104
00:08:15,291 --> 00:08:15,838
See you there.

